To determine the heat transferred (\(Q\)) when 250 J of work is done **on** a system (1 mole of ideal gas) at constant temperature (isothermal process), we use the first law of thermodynamics:  
\[
\Delta E = Q - W_{\text{by system}}
\]  
**Key steps:**  
1. **Isothermal Condition:** For an ideal gas, \(\Delta E = 0\) at constant temperature.  
2. **Work Sign Convention:** \(W_{\text{by system}}\) is positive when the system does work (expansion). If work is **done on the system**, \(W_{\text{by system}} = -W_{\text{on system}}\).  
   Here, \(W_{\text{on system}} = 250 \, \text{J}\), so \(W_{\text{by system}} = -250 \, \text{J}\).  
3. **First Law Application:**  
   \[
   0 = Q - (-250 \, \text{J}) \implies Q = -250 \, \text{J}.
   \]  

**Conclusion:**  
The heat transferred is **-250 J**, indicating heat is released by the system.  

**Answer is C.**
