The equivalent equivalent conductance (Λ) of the solution is given as 0.0412 m²/(Ω·mol), and the transference number of the cation (t₊) is 0.825. The transference number relates to the ionic contributions as follows:

\[
t₊ = \frac{\lambda₊}{\lambda₊ + \lambda₋} \quad \text{and} \quad t₋ = 1 - t₊
\]

Given \( t₊ = 0.825 \), we find \( t₋ = 0.175 \). The equivalent conductance is the sum of the ionic contributions:

\[
\lambda₊ + \lambda₋ = \Lambda = 0.0412
\]

Using the transference number for the cation:

\[
\lambda₊ = t₊ \cdot \Lambda = 0.825 \times 0.0412 = 0.03399 \, \text{m²/(Ω·mol)}
\]

Approximating, \( \lambda₊ \approx 0.0340 \). Substituting back to find \( \lambda₋ \):

\[
\lambda₋ = \Lambda - \lambda₊ = 0.0412 - 0.0340 = 0.0072 \, \text{m²/(Ω·mol)}
\]

Thus, the contributions are \( \lambda₊ = 0.0340 \) and \( \lambda₋ = 0.0072 \), corresponding to **Option C**.

The answer is \boxed{C}
