The characteristic of monopolistic competition is that firms set prices above marginal cost (P > MC). This occurs because each firm faces a downward-sloping demand curve due to product differentiation. To maximize profit, they equate marginal revenue (MR) with marginal cost (MC), but since MR lies below the demand curve, the price (P) charged exceeds MR, leading to P > MC. Other options like perfect competition (A, B), efficiency (C), inelastic demand (D), perfect knowledge (F), and single seller (I) do not apply. While there is some non-price competition (e.g., advertising), the key distinguishing feature is the price-setting behavior relative to MC. 

