The improper integral \( G(s) = \int_0^\infty e^{-st} \, dt \) converges when the real part of \( s \), denoted \( \text{Re}(s) \), is positive. This is because the exponential decay \( e^{-t \cdot \text{Re}(s)} \) ensures the integrand diminishes to zero as \( t \to \infty \). For \( \text{Re}(s) \leq 0 \), the integral diverges due to oscillatory behavior (when \( \text{Re}(s) = 0 \)) or exponential growth (when \( \text{Re}(s) < 0 \)).

Evaluating the convergent integral:
\[
\int_0^\infty e^{-st} \, dt = \left[ -\frac{1}{s} e^{-st} \right]_0^\infty = \frac{1}{s}.
\]
Thus, the Laplace transform of \( f(t) = 1 \) is \( \mathcal{L}\{1\} = \frac{1}{s} \) for \( \text{Re}(s) > 0 \).

**Answer is C.**
