To solve the system of linear equations using Cramer's rule, we start by identifying the coefficient matrix \( A \) and the constants vector \( b \):

\[
A = \begin{bmatrix}
2 & 3 & 1 \\
4 & 4 & 4 \\
1 & 1 & 8
\end{bmatrix}, \quad b = \begin{bmatrix}
8 \\
12 \\
10
\end{bmatrix}
\]

First, we calculate the determinant of matrix \( A \):

\[
\det(A) = 2 \cdot (4 \cdot 8 - 4 \cdot 1) - 3 \cdot (4 \cdot 8 - 4 \cdot 1) + 1 \cdot (4 \cdot 1 - 4 \cdot 1)
\]
\[
= 2 \cdot (32 - 4) - 3 \cdot (32 - 4) + 1 \cdot (4 - 4)
\]
\[
= 2 \cdot 28 - 3 \cdot 28 + 0
\]
\[
= 56 - 84 = -28
\]

Next, we replace each column of \( A \) with \( b \) to form matrices \( A_x \), \( A_y \), and \( A_z \), and calculate their determinants:

For \( A_x \):

\[
A_x = \begin{bmatrix}
8 & 3 & 1 \\
12 & 4 & 4 \\
10 & 1 & 8
\end{bmatrix}
\]
\[
\det(A_x) = 8 \cdot (4 \cdot 8 - 4 \cdot 1) - 3 \cdot (12 \cdot 8 - 4 \cdot 10) + 1 \cdot (12 \cdot 1 - 4 \cdot 10)
\]
\[
= 8 \cdot 28 - 3 \cdot 56 + 1 \cdot (-28)
\]
\[
= 224 - 168 - 28 = 28
\]
\[
x = \frac{\det(A_x)}{\det(A)} = \frac{28}{-28} = -1
\]

For \( A_y \):

\[
A_y = \begin{bmatrix}
2 & 8 & 1 \\
4 & 12 & 4 \\
1 & 10 & 8
\end{bmatrix}
\]
\[
\det(A_y) = 2 \cdot (12 \cdot 8 - 4 \cdot 10) - 8 \cdot (4 \cdot 8 - 4 \cdot 1) + 1 \cdot (4 \cdot 10 - 12 \cdot 1)
\]
\[
= 2 \cdot 56 - 8 \cdot 28 + 1 \cdot 28
\]
\[
= 112 - 224 + 28 = -84
\]
\[
y = \frac{\det(A_y)}{\det(A)} = \frac{-84}{-28} = 3
\]

For \( A_z \):

\[
A_z = \begin{bmatrix}
2 & 3 & 8 \\
4 & 4 & 12 \\
1 & 1 & 10
\end{bmatrix}
\]
\[
\det(A_z) = 2 \cdot (4 \cdot 10 - 12 \cdot 1) - 3 \cdot (4 \cdot 10 - 12 \cdot 1) + 8 \cdot (4 \cdot 1 - 4 \cdot 1)
\]
\[
= 2 \cdot 28 - 3 \cdot 28 + 8 \cdot 0
\]
\[
= 56 - 84 = -28
\]
\[
z = \frac{\det(A_z)}{\det(A)} = \frac{-28}{-28} = 1
\]

The solution is \( x = -1 \), \( y = 3 \), and \( z = 1 \), which corresponds to option D.

\[
