The speed of the proton can be determined using the relationship between the magnetic force and centripetal force. When a charged particle moves perpendicular to a magnetic field, the radius of its circular path is given by:

\[
r = \frac{mv}{qB} \implies v = \frac{qBr}{m}
\]

**Given values**:
- Charge of proton, \( q = 1.6 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C} \)
- Magnetic field, \( B = 1.0 \, \text{Wb/m} \)
- Radius, \( r = 0.22 \, \text{m} \)
- Mass of proton, \( m = 1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg} \)

**Calculation**:
\[
v = \frac{(1.6 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(1.0 \, \text{T})(0.22 \, \text{m})}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = \frac{3.52 \times 10^{-20}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27}} \approx 2.11 \times 10^7 \, \text{m/s}
\]

**Significant figures**: The least precise values (radius and magnetic field) have two significant figures, so the final answer is rounded to \( 2.1 \times 10^7 \, \text{m/s} \).

**Answer is option I.**  
\boxed{I}
